{
  "module": "Position: where tokens are in the sequence",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "absolute-positions",
    "title": "Adding a position vector: sinusoidal and learned",
    "summary": "Two ways to give each position its own vector and add it to the token embedding at the input: a fixed sine and cosine formula, and a learned table.",
    "covers": ["sinusoidal-position", "learned-absolute-position"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "position-need",
        "reason": "These are two fixes for the problem it describes."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "embeddings",
        "reason": "The position vector is added to the token embedding, and the learned version is a table like the embedding table."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "add-a-vector",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The first family of fixes gives every "
            },
            {
              "text": "position",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " its own vector, the same length as a token embedding, and "
            },
            {
              "text": "adds",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " it to the token's embedding at the input."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "x_{\\mathrm{pos}} = E[\\mathrm{token}] + P_{\\mathrm{pos}}",
          "spoken": "x at position pos equals the embedding of the token plus the position vector for pos"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Now the token "
            },
            {
              "text": "dog",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " at position 2 and "
            },
            {
              "text": "dog",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " at position 9 start out with different vectors. The schemes differ in where the position vectors come from."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 46, 47)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "sinusoidal",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Sinusoidal",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " encoding, from the original Transformer, computes them with a fixed formula of sines and cosines. Position pos and vector entry 2i and 2i + 1 give:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "PE(pos, 2i) = \\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d_{\\mathrm{model}}}}\\right) \\qquad PE(pos, 2i{+}1) = \\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/d_{\\mathrm{model}}}}\\right)",
          "spoken": "P E of pos and 2 i equals sine of pos over 10000 to the power 2 i over d model. P E of pos and 2 i plus 1 equals cosine of the same"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It is "
            },
            {
              "text": "fixed, not learned",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": nothing in it is a parameter. The vector is added elementwise to the token embedding."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 46)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "sinusoidal-toy",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy case with d_model = 4. The entries are (sin, cos) of pos, then (sin, cos) of pos / 100:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "position 0: (0.000, 1.000, 0.000, 1.000)"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "position 1: (0.841, 0.540, 0.010, 1.000)"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "position 2: (0.909, -0.416, 0.020, 1.000)"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each position gets a different vector. The first pair changes quickly with position and the second pair changes slowly. The wavelengths across the pairs form a geometric progression, so the vector works like a set of clocks running at different speeds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The original authors hypothesised that this design would help the model attend by relative offset, since PE(pos + k) is a linear function of PE(pos). That is their hypothesis, not a proven property."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 46)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "learned",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Learned absolute position embeddings",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", as in GPT-2, take the other route: a table P of shape "
            },
            {
              "text": "n_ctx × d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", learned by training like every other parameter. Row pos is added to the token embedding."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "GPT-2 used n_ctx = 1024. With d_model = 768, the table has 1024 × 768 = 786,432 parameters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 47)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "limit",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A learned table has a limit built in: there is "
            },
            {
              "text": "no row for positions beyond the context length it was trained with",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". Text longer than n_ctx has no natural position vectors, so there is no natural way to extrapolate to longer inputs."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is one of the things that limits how long a context can be."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 47)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "added-once",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Both schemes are "
            },
            {
              "text": "absolute",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": a position gets its vector from its number alone. And both add it "
            },
            {
              "text": "once, at the input",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". After that, position travels along with the token's vector into the later layers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The next lesson shows a scheme that works differently: it adds nothing to the input at all."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 50)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
